   7. Bayesian Hierarchical Spatial \tab \verb{bhs_*} \tab implemented \tab Hierarchical spatial linear model \eqn{y = X\beta + w + \varepsilon} with \eqn{w \sim GP(0, \sigma^2 \rho(\cdot; \phi))} and \eqn{\varepsilon \sim N(0, \tau^2 I)}; conjugate Gibbs sampler (\code{\link[=bhs_fit]{bhs_fit()}}) whose \eqn{\beta} and variance updates are closed-form, with the range \eqn{\phi} handled by profile-MLE over a user grid — the same hyperparameter strategy as INLA (Rue, Martino and Chopin, 2009) — and an RcppArmadillo backend for the Cholesky-heavy inner loop. Bayesian kriging at new sites (\code{\link[=predict.edaphos_bhs]{predict.edaphos_bhs()}}) propagates every posterior draw of \eqn{(\beta, \sigma^2, \tau^2)} into the predictive distribution. Rank-deficient designs are rejected up front with the aliased column named. \cr
   8. Neural Operators \tab \verb{no_*} \tab implemented \tab Learns the solution operator \eqn{\mathcal{G}: u(z) \mapsto y(z)} between depth-function spaces rather than a pointwise regression. Two architectures: a 1-D \strong{Fourier Neural Operator} (\code{\link[=no_fno_fit]{no_fno_fit()}}) whose blocks act as \eqn{(\mathcal{K}v)(z) = \mathcal{F}^{-1}(R_\theta \cdot \mathcal{F}v)(z)} with the spectrum truncated to \code{n_modes}, and a \strong{DeepONet} (\code{\link[=no_deeponet_fit]{no_deeponet_fit()}}) factorising \eqn{\mathcal{G}(u)(z) \approx \sum_{k=1}^{p} b_k(u)\, t_k(z)} into branch and trunk networks, which is the constructive form of the Chen and Chen (1995) universal operator approximation theorem. Both ship a pure-R backend and a \code{torch} backend with full autograd and cpu / mps / cuda dispatch. \cr
   9. Diffusion Models \tab \verb{dm_*} \tab implemented \tab Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Model (Ho, Jain and Abbeel, 2020) over soil-map patches. Forward process \eqn{q(x_t \mid x_0) = N(\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}\, x_0, (1-\bar\alpha_t) I)} on the cosine schedule of Nichol and Dhariwal (2021) (\code{\link[=dm_cosine_schedule]{dm_cosine_schedule()}}); a denoiser \eqn{\epsilon_\theta(x_t, t)} trained by score matching (\code{\link[=dm_fit]{dm_fit()}}); ancestral sampling from \eqn{t = T} down to 0 (\code{\link[=dm_sample]{dm_sample()}}), optionally conditioned on covariates. The \code{backend = "r"} trainer updates the readout layer only over random hidden features (an ELM regime, Huang, Zhu and Siew, 2006); \code{backend = "torch"} trains the whole network. \cr
   10. Graph Neural Networks \tab \verb{gnn_*} \tab implemented \tab Graph Attention Network (Veli\enc{č}{c}kovi\enc{ć}{c} et al., 2018) over a k-NN co-location graph of profiles (\code{\link[=gnn_build_graph]{gnn_build_graph()}}, with \code{edges = "spatial"}, \code{"feature"} or \code{"hybrid"} and optional self-loops). \code{\link[=gnn_fit]{gnn_fit()}} trains multi-head attention \eqn{\alpha_{ij} = \mathrm{softmax}_j(\mathrm{LeakyReLU}(a^\top [Wh_i \Vert Wh_j]))} by analytic reverse-mode gradients through the grouped softmax — verified against central finite differences — with residual connections, head averaging, a transductive \code{train_mask} and a closed-form ridge readout. \code{\link[=gnn_fit_ensemble]{gnn_fit_ensemble()}} averages seeds so the comparison against a quantile regression forest is between two ensembles rather than one model and a forest; \code{\link[=gnn_embed]{gnn_embed()}} exposes the learned node embeddings and \code{\link[=gnn_causal_discovery]{gnn_causal_discovery()}} feeds them to Pillar 1's structure learner. \cr
